A thin, opinionated wrapper around \code{\link[ggbreak:scale_y_break]{ggbreak::scale_y_break()}} and
\code{\link[ggbreak:scale_x_break]{ggbreak::scale_x_break()}} that auto-detects outlier gaps from plot data.
A gap is defined by a consecutive ratio: if the ratio of a sorted value to
the next smaller value exceeds \code{cutoff}, the region between them is cut from
the axis and replaced by a zigzag break symbol. Multiple gaps produce
multiple breaks.

The main data cluster (the segment with the most unique values) is shown at
full scale; outlier values appear in a compressed secondary panel joined by
the break symbol.
}
\section{Limitations}{

\itemize{
\item Outlier detection operates on all data values across all layers
collectively (positive values only).
\item Works best with linear scales; log-transformed scales may produce
unexpected break positions.
\item Requires the \strong{ggbreak} package (listed under \code{Suggests}).
\item Line geoms that cross a break are visually cut at the break boundary
but no additional break symbol is drawn on the line itself.
}
}

